1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,740
Meet our passenger.

2
00:00:01,740 --> 00:00:03,680
They want to visit a
friend in another city,

3
00:00:03,680 --> 00:00:06,430
and the airline's website says
the flight costs twenty dollars.

4
00:00:06,930 --> 00:00:08,220
Twenty dollars, to fly.

5
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:09,200
So they book it.

6
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,330
Then the checkout keeps going.

7
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:13,210
A cabin bag: $25.

8
00:00:13,210 --> 00:00:15,100
A window seat: $12.

9
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,370
A checked suitcase: $35.

10
00:00:17,870 --> 00:00:20,120
The twenty-dollar flight now costs $92.

11
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:22,360
Those prices are a made-up example.

12
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:23,580
The pattern is real.

13
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:26,320
At the American budget airline Frontier,

14
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:30,900
the average passenger in
2025 paid $44.60 in fares,

15
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:34,190
and another $63.79 in other charges.

16
00:00:34,690 --> 00:00:36,960
So how can an airline sell
a flight for twenty dollars?

17
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:38,980
And who is really paying for it?

18
00:00:40,280 --> 00:00:42,860
To find out, follow our
passenger onto the plane,

19
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:45,210
and look at the bill
for the flight itself.

20
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:49,990
Europe's largest budget airline,
Ryanair, publishes that bill every year.

21
00:00:49,990 --> 00:00:53,840
In the twelve months to March 2026,
it carried 208 million passengers.

22
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:59,900
Averaged over all of them, flying
one passenger cost Ryanair €63.21.

23
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:05,870
The biggest slice is fuel: €26,
about 41 percent of all its costs.

24
00:01:05,870 --> 00:01:07,660
Then staff, about €9.

25
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:09,840
Airport and handling fees, about €8.50.

26
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,610
The aircraft themselves, about €6.60.

27
00:01:12,610 --> 00:01:15,420
Air traffic control charges, about €6.30.

28
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:17,880
And then sales, maintenance
and everything else.

29
00:01:18,380 --> 00:01:20,470
Fuel alone is close to half of that bill.

30
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:24,190
Remember that: when oil prices
jump, budget airlines feel it first.

31
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:28,430
Now look at what the average
passenger paid for the ticket: €50.66.

32
00:01:28,430 --> 00:01:33,360
And Ryanair counts checked bag fees
and change fees inside that figure.

33
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:36,640
So on the fare alone, the average
passenger is flown at a loss.

34
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,440
The gap gets closed somewhere else.

35
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:40,600
To see where, we need three secrets.

36
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:42,570
Secret one.

37
00:01:42,570 --> 00:01:45,860
Most of what a flight costs does
not depend on who is sitting in it.

38
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:49,700
The crew gets paid, the fuel gets
burned, and the airport sends its bill,

39
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:51,700
whether the plane is full or half empty.

40
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:54,840
Ryanair's own annual report says the
operating costs of each flight do

41
00:01:54,840 --> 00:01:57,550
not vary significantly with
the number of passengers flown.

42
00:01:58,050 --> 00:02:02,410
Averaged across the year, one Ryanair
flight cost about €11,400 to operate,

43
00:02:02,410 --> 00:02:05,170
and carried about 181 passengers.

44
00:02:05,670 --> 00:02:09,130
And once the door closes, every empty seat
around our passenger is gone for good.

45
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:12,020
Like a concert ticket after the
show, or a hotel room after midnight,

46
00:02:12,020 --> 00:02:13,490
it can never be sold again.

47
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:17,680
One more passenger, though, adds only a
little: some per-passenger fees and taxes,

48
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:19,000
and a bit of extra fuel.

49
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,630
So for the airline, a cheap
seat beats an empty one.

50
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:23,260
That changes the question.

51
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:25,510
It is not, what does one seat cost?

52
00:02:25,510 --> 00:02:27,790
It is, how full does the plane need to be?

53
00:02:28,290 --> 00:02:30,090
Ryanair calls that its
break-even load factor.

54
00:02:30,090 --> 00:02:33,050
In its last financial
year, it was 80 percent.

55
00:02:33,050 --> 00:02:35,490
Its planes were actually 94 percent full.

56
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:38,890
Picture one of its Boeing
jets, with 197 seats.

57
00:02:38,890 --> 00:02:41,760
Roughly the first 158
passengers pay for the flight.

58
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:44,250
The next 27 or so are where the profit is.

59
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:46,440
Secret two.

60
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:49,650
The twenty-dollar seats are real,
but there are not many of them.

61
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:52,710
Budget airlines change
their prices constantly.

62
00:02:52,710 --> 00:02:56,470
Ryanair says its fares vary with how far
ahead you book, how many seats are left,

63
00:02:56,470 --> 00:02:57,270
and demand.

64
00:02:57,770 --> 00:02:59,950
And because it sells
every journey one way,

65
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:03,080
each seat on each flight
can carry its own price.

66
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:05,670
Think of every flight
as a ladder of prices.

67
00:03:05,670 --> 00:03:08,120
Our passenger booked weeks
ahead, for a quiet Tuesday,

68
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:09,860
and landed on the bottom rung.

69
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:12,970
As the plane fills up,
each new seat costs more.

70
00:03:12,970 --> 00:03:16,540
The person in the next seat, who booked
yesterday, may have paid many times as much,

71
00:03:16,540 --> 00:03:18,030
for exactly the same flight.

72
00:03:18,530 --> 00:03:20,170
Those cheap seats do a job.

73
00:03:20,170 --> 00:03:21,560
They fill the plane early,

74
00:03:21,560 --> 00:03:24,070
they put the airline's name at
the top of every price comparison,

75
00:03:24,070 --> 00:03:25,660
and they bring you to its website.

76
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:30,160
Ryanair even has a name for this:
load factor active, yield passive.

77
00:03:30,160 --> 00:03:34,380
In plain words: fill the plane first, and
worry about the price of each seat second.

78
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:36,940
It admits that promotional
fares can lower its

79
00:03:36,940 --> 00:03:38,640
revenue on those routes while they run.

80
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:41,380
It keeps running them anyway,
to fill quiet periods.

81
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:43,440
Secret three.

82
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:45,960
A budget fare buys you
less than you might think.

83
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:48,960
At the gate, our passenger finds
out what the fare included.

84
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:52,430
Usually, a seat and one small bag
that fits under the seat in front.

85
00:03:52,430 --> 00:03:55,830
Almost everything else is sold
separately: a bigger bag, a suitcase,

86
00:03:55,830 --> 00:04:00,210
choosing your seat, boarding early, changing
your ticket, even a drink on board.

87
00:04:00,710 --> 00:04:03,470
At Ryanair, extras like priority boarding,

88
00:04:03,470 --> 00:04:07,750
reserved seats and onboard sales
brought in €4.99 billion last year.

89
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:12,000
That is about €24 per passenger, and
roughly a third of all its revenue.

90
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:17,800
Add the fare and the extras together,
and Ryanair took in €74.60 per passenger,

91
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:20,010
against a cost of €63.21.

92
00:04:20,010 --> 00:04:23,430
That gap, about €11 a
passenger, is the profit.

93
00:04:23,930 --> 00:04:27,930
In the United States, the
split can be even more extreme.

94
00:04:27,930 --> 00:04:31,310
Frontier's passengers paid more
for extras than for their tickets:

95
00:04:31,310 --> 00:04:37,360
$63.79 in non-fare charges,
against $44.60 in fares.

96
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:40,260
And Frontier's revenue
from extras grew from

97
00:04:40,260 --> 00:04:46,720
$60.55 per passenger in
2021 to $67.57 in 2025.

98
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:48,520
That is the trick behind
the twenty-dollar headline.

99
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:53,400
The airline competes on the one number
you compare across websites, the fare,

100
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:55,820
and earns its money on
the numbers you see later.

101
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,290
For someone with one small
backpack, that is a genuine bargain.

102
00:04:59,290 --> 00:05:01,980
For a family with
suitcases, it might not be.

103
00:05:03,280 --> 00:05:05,140
But none of that works
unless the airline's

104
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,490
own costs stay far below everyone else's.

105
00:05:07,990 --> 00:05:15,390
Ryanair fits 197 seats into its newest
Boeing 737s, and 189 into the older ones.

106
00:05:15,390 --> 00:05:17,250
Traditional airlines fly the same Boeing

107
00:05:17,250 --> 00:05:21,480
737-800 with about 162
seats in two classes.

108
00:05:21,980 --> 00:05:26,560
More seats on the same plane, with the
same crew, means every seat costs less.

109
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:29,700
Its aircraft fly about nine and
a half hours a day on average,

110
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:32,140
because a plane on the
ground earns nothing.

111
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:36,190
So between flights, the clock is
running: passengers off, a quick clean,

112
00:05:36,190 --> 00:05:38,710
the next passengers on,
and back in the air.

113
00:05:39,210 --> 00:05:43,490
Almost the whole fleet is one aircraft
family, the Boeing 737, so pilots,

114
00:05:43,490 --> 00:05:45,680
spare parts and mechanics
are interchangeable.

115
00:05:46,180 --> 00:05:50,070
It looks for airports that charge less,
uses outdoor stairs instead of jet bridges,

116
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:52,640
and makes every passenger check in online.

117
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:58,540
The result: leaving out fuel, Ryanair
puts its own costs at €36 per passenger.

118
00:05:58,540 --> 00:06:02,070
It puts Wizz Air at €62, easyJet at €90,

119
00:06:02,070 --> 00:06:06,750
and the big network airline
groups at €178 to €253.

120
00:06:07,250 --> 00:06:09,290
That cost gap is the real product.

121
00:06:09,290 --> 00:06:11,760
The twenty-dollar fare is just
how it shows up on your screen.

122
00:06:13,060 --> 00:06:14,330
The model has a weak spot.

123
00:06:14,330 --> 00:06:18,170
It needs full planes, steady fuel
prices, and very little going wrong.

124
00:06:18,670 --> 00:06:22,070
Ryanair needed its planes about 80
percent full just to break even.

125
00:06:22,070 --> 00:06:25,980
In 2025, Frontier's planes
were 78.4 percent full.

126
00:06:26,480 --> 00:06:30,730
Frontier's costs came to
about $116.66 per passenger,

127
00:06:30,730 --> 00:06:34,190
a little more than the $112.17 it took in.

128
00:06:34,190 --> 00:06:36,900
It lost $137 million that year.

129
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:41,010
Spirit Airlines, which described itself
as a pioneer of affordable travel,

130
00:06:41,010 --> 00:06:45,550
filed for bankruptcy in November
2024, and again in August 2025.

131
00:06:46,050 --> 00:06:49,660
On May 2, 2026, citing a
sharp rise in fuel prices,

132
00:06:49,660 --> 00:06:52,500
it cancelled all of its
flights and began winding down.

133
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,680
When costs jump, a budget airline
cannot simply raise its fares.

134
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:59,300
Low fares are the whole
reason people fly with it.

135
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:02,750
That is why Ryanair locks in
fuel prices ahead of time.

136
00:07:02,750 --> 00:07:07,320
It says about 80 percent of its fuel for
the year to March 2027 is already hedged:

137
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:09,840
a cushion that weaker
rivals often do not have.

138
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:12,360
And when one of these airlines disappears,

139
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:15,700
the people who relied on it lose
the cheapest way they had to travel.

140
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,130
So, how can an airline sell
a flight for twenty dollars?

141
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:23,500
Because an empty seat is worth
nothing once the plane leaves,

142
00:07:23,500 --> 00:07:26,420
and one more passenger
costs very little to carry.

143
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:29,720
Because the cheapest seats are a
handful at the bottom of a price ladder,

144
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:31,800
and people who book later pay much more.

145
00:07:32,300 --> 00:07:34,580
Because the fare is only
part of what you pay.

146
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:36,970
Bags, seats and extras close the gap.

147
00:07:37,470 --> 00:07:40,220
And because the airline
is built, piece by piece,

148
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:43,210
to fly each seat more
cheaply than its rivals.

149
00:07:43,710 --> 00:07:44,970
So who pays for the twenty-dollar seat?

150
00:07:44,970 --> 00:07:46,570
Partly the people who book late.

151
00:07:46,570 --> 00:07:48,010
Partly the people who add a suitcase.

152
00:07:48,010 --> 00:07:50,680
And partly the airline's
obsession with cost.

153
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:53,200
If you travel light, book
early and skip the extras,

154
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:54,860
you might be the one getting the deal.

155
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:56,630
This is Hidden Ledger.

156
00:07:56,630 --> 00:07:59,170
Every number in this video is
sourced in the description.

